{
  "dom_id": "step-q352915",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
